<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
   <!--
     CVE-2026-53914 (NVD-rescored CVSS 9.8; original JetBrains vector is CVSS 6.7,
     AV:L/AC:H/PR:H/UI:N) - "In JetBrains Kotlin before 2.4.20 code execution was
     possible via unsafe deserialization in the build cache metadata."

     This flags every org.jetbrains.kotlin:kotlin-stdlib* / kotlin-reflect jar on
     our classpath (currently resolved at 1.9.25, transitively pulled in by the
     com.anthropic:anthropic-java SDK's OkHttp/Jackson-Kotlin integration).

     Why this does not apply to BrewDeck:
       - The vulnerable code path is Kotlin's INCREMENTAL COMPILATION BUILD CACHE
         (deserialization of cached build metadata written by the Kotlin compiler /
         Kotlin Gradle plugin during a Kotlin build). BrewDeck is built with Maven
         and never invokes the Kotlin compiler, the Kotlin Gradle plugin, or any
         Kotlin build-cache feature - Kotlin appears here purely as a transitive
         *runtime* library (kotlin-stdlib/-jdk7/-jdk8/-reflect) consumed by
         precompiled classes inside the Anthropic Java SDK. The attack surface
         described by the advisory (a local, high-privilege actor tampering with
         build-cache metadata on a Kotlin build machine) does not exist in
         BrewDeck's build or runtime environment.

     Why this is suppressed instead of fixed:
       - The fixed line, Kotlin 2.4.20, has not reached a stable (GA) release as
         of this review (only 2.4.20-Beta1/-Beta2 exist on Maven Central); BrewDeck
         policy disallows adopting beta/RC dependencies. There is no stable
         kotlin-stdlib release >= 2.4.20 to upgrade to yet.

     Owner: dependency-upgrade-engineer (via security.yml scheduled scan)
     Follow-up: remove this suppression and upgrade kotlin-stdlib/kotlin-reflect
     (directly, or by tracking a future com.anthropic:anthropic-java release that
     manages a fixed Kotlin version) once Kotlin 2.4.20 (or later) reaches GA.
     Re-verify at that time whether anthropic-java still pulls a vulnerable
     Kotlin runtime.
   -->
   <!--
     Round 3 (2026-09-22) update: Kotlin 2.4.20 reached a stable GA release (previously
     only Beta/RC), so kotlin-stdlib, kotlin-stdlib-jdk7, kotlin-stdlib-jdk8, and
     kotlin-reflect are now pinned to 2.4.20 via a dependencyManagement override in
     pom.xml and are no longer affected by this CVE (validated by a full
     Testcontainers-backed `./mvnw clean verify`). kotlin-stdlib-common could NOT be
     bumped: as of 2.4.20 it publishes packaging=pom only, with no jar artifact
     (confirmed: 404 on its jar coordinate, 200 on its .pom, on Maven Central) -
     forcing the override broke dependency resolution entirely, since okio-jvm expects
     an actual jar. kotlin-stdlib-common therefore stays unmanaged and remains resolved
     at the original transitive version (1.9.25), so this suppression is narrowed to
     that single artifact rather than removed.

     Re-checked 2026-10-19 (OPS-02): still needed. kotlin-stdlib-common now resolves to
     1.9.10 (transitive) and NVD still lists the CVE as affecting Kotlin before 2.4.20.
   -->
   <suppress>
      <notes><![CDATA[
         kotlin-stdlib-common cannot be bumped past 1.9.25: as of 2.4.20 it is a
         packaging=pom artifact with no published jar (confirmed via Maven Central),
         so no fixed jar exists for it to resolve to. The underlying reachability
         argument from the original suppression above still applies unchanged: the
         vulnerable code path is Kotlin's incremental-compilation build-cache
         deserialization, never exercised by BrewDeck's Maven build.
      ]]></notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.jetbrains\.kotlin/kotlin\-stdlib\-common@.*$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2026-53914</cve>
   </suppress>

   <!--
     Post-Spring-Boot-4-migration finding (PR #117's own security.yml run, 2026-09-22):
     spring-boot-devtools 4.1.1 is flagged for CVE-2022-31691 (CVSS 9.8) via the CPE
     cpe:2.3:a:vmware:spring_boot_tools:4.1.1:*:*:*:*:*:*:*. This is a CPE false match,
     not a real finding: CVE-2022-31691's actual advisory (confirmed against public CVE
     data) targets "Spring Tools 4" for Eclipse (STS4, up to 4.16.0) and its related
     VSCode extensions (Spring Boot Tools, Concourse Pipeline Editor, Bosh Editor,
     Cloudfoundry Manifest YML Support) - IDE tooling vulnerable via SnakeYAML-based
     remote code execution in their YAML editor. It has nothing to do with
     org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-devtools, the runtime library dependency
     BrewDeck declares (LiveReload/restart-on-classpath-change support for local dev).
     The NVD CPE dictionary's "spring_boot_tools" product name collides with this
     unrelated artifact's coordinates, and dependency-check's CPE analyzer matched on
     that string alone. Independently, spring-boot-devtools is also declared
     `scope=runtime, optional=true` in pom.xml, so Spring Boot's repackage plugin
     excludes it from the executable JAR entirely - it is never present in a deployed
     BrewDeck artifact regardless.

     Owner: dependency-upgrade-engineer (via security.yml scheduled scan)
     Follow-up: none required; re-verify only if a future dependency-check CPE
     dictionary update changes this match (e.g. if NVD splits the conflated CPE).
   -->
   <suppress>
      <notes><![CDATA[
         CPE false match: CVE-2022-31691 targets Spring Tools 4 (Eclipse/VSCode IDE
         plugin), not the org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-devtools library. Also
         scope=runtime,optional=true - never packaged into the deployed JAR.
      ]]></notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/org\.springframework\.boot/spring\-boot\-devtools@.*$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2022-31691</cve>
   </suppress>
</suppressions>
